2.2.m:
And the king who has a minister endowed with such qualities and takes pleasure in his words, / his kingdom will be secure, his fortune abundant, and his lineage renowned even across the three worlds.
2.7
Puṣkara said:
A king should make his chief queen one honored with all auspicious marks, / humble, devoted to elders, and free from jealousy and anger;
devoted to what is pleasant and beneficial to the king, beautifully dressed, pleasant of speech, / knowing the dependent and non-dependent folk, and attentive to her dependents;
and promoting maintenance and work for those who are without support; / and one who is always free from passion and hatred toward co-wives;
attentive to all with food, seating, and drink; / and one who behaves toward the sons of co-wives as if toward her own son;
and who always honors the ministers, the astrologer, and the counsellors; / pious toward brahmins, compassionate, and showing mercy to all living beings;
knowing what has been done and what remains to be done, well known throughout the kingdoms of the king, / affectionate and full of joy toward the wives of other kings;

He whose two eyes, except for the pupils, are white, and whose thirty-two teeth / are likewise white, O Rāma, he is called 'white in two'.
He whose chest, navel, and thigh are deep, / that best of men, O knower of dharma, is called 'deep in three'. / Freedom from malice, compassion, and patience are proclaimed as one triad;
auspicious conduct, pleasant touch, and purity are another triad; / freedom from distress, lack of pettiness, and lordliness are three triads. / He is called 'dangling in three' by his two arms and scrotum. / Through space, country, and caste-origins, by brilliance, fame, and fortune,

A conveyance for one fatigued, food for one hungry, drink for one overcome by thirst, and protection among enemies: / the man to whom these come in due time, the best of the twice-born call fortunate on earth.
2.9 / Puṣkara said: / A praiseworthy woman is one whose limbs are all charming, who walks like an elephant in rut, / who has heavy thighs and hips, and whose eyes are like those of an impassioned dove;
whose hair is very dark, whose limbs are slender, whose voice is like a cuckoo’s, / whose waist is thin, whose body is hairless, whose complexion is lustrous and captivating;
whose feet touch the ground completely and resemble lotuses, / whose navel whorls to the right, and whose breasts are firm and close;
whose lower lip is well-proportioned, whose appearance is sweet, / whose vulva whorls to the right and resembles a leaf of the holy fig tree;
whose ankles are concealed, and whose lower-back dimples are deep-set; / the distance of whose navel is equal to the measure of a hand’s thumb;
whose calves are not bound together, whose waist is not drooping, / whose belly is not protruding, and whose eyes are not squinting;

whose hair is neither tawny nor rough; / who is not named after a tree, a river, a deity, or a mountain;
nor bearing the name of a serpent, a gandharva, a bhūta, or a preta; / who is not talkative, not greedy, not deceitful, not fond of quarrel;
not covetous, not ill-disposed, nor having a dull forehead; / who is devoted to the worship of gods, the twice-born, the siddhas, and virtuous men;
endowed with good conduct, endowed with virtues, not prominent in veins, not hairy;
with cheeks resembling madhūka flowers, with glossy lips, / without meeting eyebrows, and the buds of whose toes are closely joined.
A woman who is dear to her husband, who holds her husband as her life, and who has her husband as her deity, / even if devoid of auspicious marks, should be understood as possessing all auspicious marks.
A woman whose pupil does not touch the eyebrow in any way, / one should not take as a wife; she is declared by the wise to be death [itself].
One whose upper lip has hair, whose cheeks have dimples, / who is exceedingly tall, tawny-brown, and whose masses of flesh shake—
she is especially to be avoided by a man desiring his own welfare.

In brief, this auspicious mark of women with beautiful hips has been told to you by me. / This secret has been declared in this regard: where there is beauty of form, there virtues dwell.
2.10 / Puṣkara said: / Elephants that are superior in measurement are praised, O knower of dharma, / particularly those with long trunks, deep breath, and a good seat [for a rider];
those whose spinal ridge is concealed, who have honey-coloured eyes, who are gentle, whose lips and heads are smooth, / who have twenty-eight nails, and whose rut occurs in the cold season;
whose trunks, feet, and tails are long, / whose right tusk is raised and shining, and whose roar resembles a raincloud;
who endure severe pain, are heroic, and can bear loud sounds; / whose ears are broad, and whose skin is marked with fine spots.
Those, O fortunate one, are praised who stand firmly on seven points, / on whose lips are seen auspicious marks of the svastika,
ornamental designs, chowries, jars, and goads; / these should be kept, whereas dwarfs, tuskless elephants, and the like should not be kept;

2.3.m:
It should be specially decorated with gold, silver, and copper, / and with praised jewels—not with flawed ones, nor with imitation gems.
Four men are placed there, O descendant of Bhṛgu, / and double that number of lions, and double that again [of other figures].
There the throne of the king should be solid at the base, comfortable, and of the highest value; / they prescribe an excellent seat for him covered with a tiger skin for comfort.
2.15
Puṣkara said:
Diamond, emerald, ruby, pearl, / sapphire, deep blue sapphire, cat's-eye, and sasyaka;
indrakānta, sūryakānta, crystal, pulaka, / karketana, topaz, and jyotīrasa, O twice-born;
quartz, lapis lazuli, auspicious rājamaya, / saugandhika, sakhya, conch-shell, and brahmamaya;
gomedha, rudhirākṣa, ballātaka, O twice-born, / dust-emerald, tuttūka, and śeṣa;
